  "image_name": "58a19b2a3acd9285645978cda95db7ab.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a concept diagram for an advanced electron microscope platform that combines new hardware with software-driven feedback to enable more autonomous experiments. At the center is the microscope column, showing the beam path from the gun through a corrector and scan coils to the sample, then to several detectors, including HAADF, ADF/ABF, a pixelated detector, and EELS.\n\nOn the hardware side, the image highlights added capabilities that broaden what the instrument can do: rapid beam-energy switching for faster movement between manipulation and characterization, cathodoluminescence for optical and plasmonic measurements, a high-resolution spectrometer for plasmons, phonons, and electronic structure, a pixelated camera for 4D STEM, a variable-temperature stage, and an ion/gas source for deposition or atomic manipulation.\n\nOn the software side, detector output feeds into a pipeline involving a materials genome library, artificial intelligence data analysis, and an experimental decision logic flow. That analysis appears to send updated beam and sample control parameters back to the microscope, with a user interface overseeing the loop. Overall, the figure depicts a closed-loop, AI-assisted microscopy system designed for real-time characterization and control at the atomic scale.",
